1
00:00:04,280 --> 00:00:09,060
But he stripped himself of his rags,
Odysseus of the many designs,

2
00:00:09,060 --> 00:00:15,080
and leapt to the great threshold with the bow and the
quiver full of arrows, and poured out the swift shafts

3
00:00:15,080 --> 00:00:18,660
there at his feet, and spoke among the suitors:

4
00:00:18,660 --> 00:00:22,100
"So: that contest-past-all-contest is accomplished.

5
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:25,480
Now for another mark — one no man has ever hit —

6
00:00:25,480 --> 00:00:29,000
and I shall know if I strike it:
if Apollo gives me that glory."

7
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:32,580
He spoke, and leveled the bitter arrow at Antinous.

8
00:00:32,580 --> 00:00:39,160
And he — he was that instant lifting a beautiful cup,
a golden one, two-eared, turning it in his hands

9
00:00:39,160 --> 00:00:42,920
to drink the wine; and slaughter was not in his thoughts:

10
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:49,140
for who would think, among men at dinner, that one man
in the middle of many — however strong he might be —

11
00:00:49,140 --> 00:00:52,060
would bring on him foul death and the black doom?

12
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:57,700
But Odysseus took aim at his throat, and shot,
and the point went clean on through the soft of the neck.

13
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:03,820
He sagged to one side; the cup dropped out of his
hand as the arrow hit; and at once a thick jet came

14
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:08,180
up through his nostrils —
human blood; and quick, with a kick,

15
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:11,340
he shoved the table from him and
spilled the food to the floor:

16
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:14,940
the bread and the roast meats
fouled in it. And the suitors

17
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:18,760
broke into uproar through the
house when they saw the man fallen;

18
00:01:18,760 --> 00:01:21,940
up from their chairs they started,
driven through the hall,

19
00:01:21,940 --> 00:01:24,200
eyes darting every way along the well-built walls —

20
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:27,760
and nowhere was there a shield or a strong spear to seize.

21
00:01:27,760 --> 00:01:31,060
And they raged at Odysseus with words of fury:

22
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,640
"Stranger, you shoot at men —
and it will cost you! No more contests

23
00:01:34,640 --> 00:01:38,000
for you: now your own sheer destruction is certain.

24
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,900
The man you have killed was the
very noblest of the young men

25
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,900
of Ithaca. For this the vultures will eat you — here."

26
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:47,760
So each man guessed: for indeed they thought he had killed

27
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:51,180
the man unwilling. The fools — they had not seen it yet:

28
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:54,160
that on every one of them the
cords of death were fastened.

29
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:58,220
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

30
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:01,280
"You dogs! You kept on saying
I would never come home again

31
00:02:01,280 --> 00:02:07,120
out of the land of Troy: and so you sheared away
my house, and lay with my slave-women by force,

32
00:02:07,120 --> 00:02:12,060
and courted my own wife while I was still alive,
fearing neither the gods who hold wide heaven

33
00:02:12,060 --> 00:02:15,240
nor any vengeance of men, to come hereafter.

34
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:19,320
Now on you — on all of you —
the cords of death are fastened."

35
00:02:19,320 --> 00:02:22,540
So he spoke; and the pale terror took hold of them all,

36
00:02:22,540 --> 00:02:26,380
and every man peered round for a
way to escape sheer destruction.

37
00:02:26,380 --> 00:02:29,580
Only Eurymachus found words, and answered him:

38
00:02:29,580 --> 00:02:33,120
"If you are truly Odysseus of Ithaca, come home again,

39
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:36,300
then all you say is just — all the Achaeans have done,

40
00:02:36,300 --> 00:02:39,600
reckless things in plenty in the hall,
in plenty on the land.

41
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:42,620
But there he lies — the man to blame for everything:

42
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:47,980
Antinous. He was the one who set these doings on —
not so much needing the marriage, or wanting it,

43
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:51,780
but with another purpose,
which the son of Cronos never granted:

44
00:02:51,780 --> 00:02:56,840
to be king himself in the land of well-built
Ithaca, and to ambush your son and kill him.

45
00:02:56,840 --> 00:03:01,020
Now he is killed, as was his portion. You — spare your own

46
00:03:01,020 --> 00:03:06,920
people; and we hereafter, making it good through the land,
all that has been drunk and eaten in your halls,

47
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:12,560
each of us bringing a payment of twenty oxen's worth,
will pay you back in bronze and gold, until your heart

48
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:16,080
is warmed. Till then, no one could blame your anger."

49
00:03:16,080 --> 00:03:20,280
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

50
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:23,560
"Eurymachus — not if you handed over all your patrimonies,

51
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,480
everything you now have, and added more from elsewhere,

52
00:03:26,480 --> 00:03:32,180
not even so would I hold my hands back from the killing
before the suitors had paid for the whole trespass.

53
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:38,440
Now the choice lies before you: fight me face to face,
or run — whoever can slip his death and doom.

54
00:03:38,440 --> 00:03:41,980
But I do not think one man of
you escapes sheer destruction."

55
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:45,960
And their knees went slack where
they stood, and their hearts.

56
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:48,920
And Eurymachus called out among them a second time:

57
00:03:48,920 --> 00:03:52,660
"Friends — this man will not
now hold his invincible hands.

58
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:59,520
He has the polished bow and the quiver: he will shoot
from the smooth threshold until he has killed us all.

59
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:05,880
Then let our minds be on battle! Draw your swords,
put up the tables against the arrows of quick death,

60
00:04:05,880 --> 00:04:12,120
and let us all go at him in one body — see if we can
shove him off the threshold and out of the doors,

61
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:15,360
then through the town, and the alarm raised at once!

62
00:04:15,360 --> 00:04:18,160
Then this man would soon have shot his last."

63
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:22,760
So speaking, he drew his sharp sword,
bronze, two-edged, and leapt at him

64
00:04:22,760 --> 00:04:26,620
with a blood-freezing yell.
But in the same instant noble Odysseus

65
00:04:26,620 --> 00:04:30,280
let the arrow fly, and struck his chest beside the nipple,

66
00:04:30,280 --> 00:04:33,500
and drove the swift shaft into his liver. Out of his hand

67
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:36,360
the sword dropped to the ground; he doubled over the table

68
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:40,480
and crumpled, sprawling across it,
and spilled the food to the floor

69
00:04:40,480 --> 00:04:46,640
and the two-eared cup. His forehead beat the
earth in his heart's agony; both feet kicked out,

70
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:50,300
rattling the chair; and the
mist poured down over his eyes.

71
00:04:50,300 --> 00:04:56,040
Then Amphinomus went for glorious Odysseus,
charging him head-on, his sharp sword drawn,

72
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:59,140
to see if he might give way from the doors. But Telemachus

73
00:04:59,140 --> 00:05:02,780
was too quick: he caught him
from behind with the bronze spear

74
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,740
square between the shoulders,
and drove it through his chest,

75
00:05:05,740 --> 00:05:10,100
and he fell with a crash, and struck
the ground full with his forehead.

76
00:05:10,100 --> 00:05:13,300
And Telemachus sprang back,
leaving the long-shadowed spear

77
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:18,720
there in Amphinomus: for he feared that as he pulled
the long spear out, some Achaean might rush in

78
00:05:18,720 --> 00:05:22,040
and cut him with a sword, or strike him as he stooped.

79
00:05:22,040 --> 00:05:27,480
He ran, and came quickly to his dear father,
and standing close he spoke winged words:

80
00:05:27,480 --> 00:05:32,740
"Father — now I will bring you a shield and two spears
and a helmet all of bronze, fitted to your temples;

81
00:05:32,740 --> 00:05:39,580
and I will arm myself as I go, and give the swineherd
the same, and the cowherd. Armored is better."

82
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:42,380
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

83
00:05:42,380 --> 00:05:44,920
"Run — bring them, while I
still have arrows to fight with,

84
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:48,220
or they will force me from the doors, one man alone."

85
00:05:48,220 --> 00:05:51,480
So he spoke; and Telemachus obeyed his dear father

86
00:05:51,480 --> 00:05:54,400
and went to the storeroom where
the famous arms were lying.

87
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:59,560
He took out four shields, and eight spears,
and four bronze helmets thick with horsehair,

88
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:02,160
and came carrying them fast to his dear father.

89
00:06:02,160 --> 00:06:07,540
He girded the bronze on his own body first,
and in the same way the two slaves put on the fine armor,

90
00:06:07,540 --> 00:06:12,040
and they took their stand around Odysseus —
the deep mind, the shifting counsels.

91
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:18,180
And he, so long as he had arrows to fight with,
kept aiming, and dropping suitors in his own house,

92
00:06:18,180 --> 00:06:20,780
one man after another: they fell in heaps.

93
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:23,180
But when the arrows failed the master archer,

94
00:06:23,180 --> 00:06:26,320
he leaned the bow against the
doorpost of the strong-built hall,

95
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:32,280
to stand there by the shining entrance-wall,
and threw about his shoulders a shield of four hides,

96
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:38,220
and set on his mighty head a well-made helmet,
horsehair-crested, the plume nodding terror from the top,

97
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:41,560
and took up two strong spears, headed with bronze.

98
00:06:41,560 --> 00:06:44,660
Now there was a raised side-door in the well-built wall,

99
00:06:44,660 --> 00:06:47,340
and level with the top of the threshold of the strong hall

100
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:51,580
an exit to a passage, closed with well-fitted boards.

101
00:06:51,580 --> 00:06:56,600
Odysseus set the noble swineherd to watch it,
posted close: it was the single way out.

102
00:06:56,600 --> 00:07:00,520
And Agelaus spoke to them all,
putting the word to everyone:

103
00:07:00,520 --> 00:07:02,960
"Friends — could not someone climb through the side-door

104
00:07:02,960 --> 00:07:06,300
and get word to the people,
and the alarm be raised at once?

105
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,960
Then this man would soon have shot his last."

106
00:07:08,960 --> 00:07:11,240
Then Melanthius the goatherd answered him:

107
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:14,900
"No way, Agelaus, Zeus-nurtured:
the fine gates of the courtyard

108
00:07:14,900 --> 00:07:18,100
are terribly near, and the mouth of the passage is cruel:

109
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:22,420
one man could hold it against all comers —
any man with strength in him.

110
00:07:22,420 --> 00:07:25,340
No — wait: let me bring you armor from the storeroom

111
00:07:25,340 --> 00:07:29,000
to harness you. For it is in there,
I am certain — nowhere else —

112
00:07:29,000 --> 00:07:32,740
that Odysseus and his shining son have stowed the arms."

113
00:07:32,740 --> 00:07:35,260
So speaking, Melanthius the goatherd climbed up

114
00:07:35,260 --> 00:07:39,020
through the smoke-vents of the
hall to the storerooms of Odysseus,

115
00:07:39,020 --> 00:07:44,360
and took out twelve shields, as many spears,
as many bronze helmets thick with horsehair,

116
00:07:44,360 --> 00:07:47,660
and came back fast and gave them to the suitors.

117
00:07:47,660 --> 00:07:50,700
And then the knees of Odysseus went slack, and his heart,

118
00:07:50,700 --> 00:07:53,460
when he saw them buckling on the armor and brandishing

119
00:07:53,460 --> 00:07:57,700
the long spears in their hands:
the work now looked great to him.

120
00:07:57,700 --> 00:08:00,600
And quickly he spoke winged words to Telemachus:

121
00:08:00,600 --> 00:08:07,120
"Telemachus — one of the women in the halls is stoking
the evil war against us — that, or it is Melanthius."

122
00:08:07,120 --> 00:08:09,520
Then clear-headed Telemachus answered him:

123
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:14,720
"Father, the fault is mine — my own, no other's —
I left the storeroom's close-fitted door

124
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:17,720
standing ajar. Their lookout was sharper than I was.

125
00:08:17,720 --> 00:08:23,940
Go, noble Eumaeus: close the storeroom door,
and find out whether it is one of the women doing this,

126
00:08:23,940 --> 00:08:28,160
or Dolius' son Melanthius — he is the one I think it is."

127
00:08:28,160 --> 00:08:30,520
So they spoke to one another of such things.

128
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:33,480
And Melanthius the goatherd went again to the storeroom

129
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:37,720
to bring out more fine armor.
But the noble swineherd marked him,

130
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,700
and quickly said to Odysseus, standing near:

131
00:08:40,700 --> 00:08:44,840
"Zeus-born son of Laertes, Odysseus of the many devices —

132
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:48,600
there he goes again, that ruinous man, the one we guessed:

133
00:08:48,600 --> 00:08:50,740
into the storeroom. Tell me straight out:

134
00:08:50,740 --> 00:08:56,540
do I kill him — if I prove the better man —
or bring him here to you, to pay for the trespasses,

135
00:08:56,540 --> 00:08:59,060
the many, that man has schemed in your house?"

136
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:02,000
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered him:

137
00:09:02,000 --> 00:09:07,660
"I and Telemachus will hold the lordly suitors
penned inside the hall, rage as they may.

138
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:14,000
You two: twist his feet and hands behind him,
throw him into the storeroom, tie boards fast at his back,

139
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:19,060
make a braided rope fast to him, and haul him
up the tall pillar, up close to the roof-beams —

140
00:09:19,060 --> 00:09:22,360
so that he lives on a long while, suffering hard pains."

141
00:09:22,360 --> 00:09:24,680
So he spoke; and they listened well and obeyed:

142
00:09:24,680 --> 00:09:28,080
they went to the storeroom —
the man inside did not see them —

143
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:31,580
and there he was, hunting armor in the room's far corner,

144
00:09:31,580 --> 00:09:35,080
while the two of them stood waiting
either side of the doorposts.

145
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:40,240
And as Melanthius the goatherd came over the threshold,
in one hand carrying a beautiful helmet,

146
00:09:40,240 --> 00:09:44,120
in the other a broad shield,
an old one, mottled with mildew —

147
00:09:44,120 --> 00:09:47,660
the hero Laertes' shield,
which he carried in his young days,

148
00:09:47,660 --> 00:09:50,820
long since laid by,
the stitches of its straps rotted out —

149
00:09:50,820 --> 00:09:54,480
the two of them sprang and seized him,
and dragged him in by the hair,

150
00:09:54,480 --> 00:09:57,280
and threw him to the floor for all his anguish of heart,

151
00:09:57,280 --> 00:09:59,980
and bound his feet and hands with a heart-grieving knot,

152
00:09:59,980 --> 00:10:05,840
wrenched hard behind him, through and through,
as ordered by Laertes' son, much-enduring noble Odysseus;

153
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:11,380
then they made a braided rope fast to him, and hauled
him up the tall pillar, up close to the roof-beams.

154
00:10:11,380 --> 00:10:14,420
And taunting him you spoke, Eumaeus the swineherd:

155
00:10:14,420 --> 00:10:18,060
"Now you will keep watch indeed,
Melanthius — the whole night through,

156
00:10:18,060 --> 00:10:23,540
stretched out on the sort of soft bed you deserve;
and the early-born one out of Ocean's streams will not

157
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:27,060
slip past you as she comes,
on her golden throne, at the hour

158
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:30,820
you drive in goats for the suitors,
to make their feast in the hall."

159
00:10:30,820 --> 00:10:36,860
So he was left there, racked in the deadly binding;
and the two got into their armor, shut the shining door,

160
00:10:36,860 --> 00:10:41,060
and went back to Odysseus —
the deep mind, the shifting counsels.

161
00:10:41,060 --> 00:10:47,220
There they stood, breathing fury: on the threshold
four men; inside the hall, many men, and good ones.

162
00:10:47,220 --> 00:10:53,060
And now Athena, daughter of Zeus, came near them,
likened to Mentor in body and in voice.

163
00:10:53,060 --> 00:10:55,400
Odysseus was glad at the sight of her, and said:

164
00:10:55,400 --> 00:11:01,340
"Mentor — beat off ruin! Remember your old companion,
who did you good service — and you my own age-mate!"

165
00:11:01,340 --> 00:11:05,180
So he spoke, guessing it was Athena, rouser of armies.

166
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:11,240
And the suitors on their side bellowed through the hall,
Agelaus, Damastor's son, first to threaten her:

167
00:11:11,240 --> 00:11:15,480
"Mentor — don't let Odysseus talk you around
into fighting the suitors and defending him.

168
00:11:15,480 --> 00:11:18,740
For here is how I think our purpose will be accomplished:

169
00:11:18,740 --> 00:11:23,640
when we have killed these two, father and son,
you will be killed with them — for the things you are minded

170
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:27,120
to do in this hall. Your own head will pay the price.

171
00:11:27,120 --> 00:11:32,180
And when the bronze has stripped the force from
all of you, everything you own, indoors and out,

172
00:11:32,180 --> 00:11:37,200
we will pool with the goods of Odysseus. Your sons
we will not let live in your halls; your daughters

173
00:11:37,200 --> 00:11:41,140
and your true-hearted wife will not
walk free in the town of Ithaca."

174
00:11:41,140 --> 00:11:47,020
So he spoke; and Athena's heart grew angrier yet,
and she lashed Odysseus with words of fury:

175
00:11:47,020 --> 00:11:51,120
"Odysseus — where is your strength gone?
Where is the fight in you —

176
00:11:51,120 --> 00:11:54,460
you who for Helen's sake,
the white-armed, the nobly fathered,

177
00:11:54,460 --> 00:11:59,340
nine years on end battled the Trojans without a
break and killed many men in the terrible fighting?

178
00:11:59,340 --> 00:12:03,560
By your counsel the wide-wayed city of Priam was taken!

179
00:12:03,560 --> 00:12:06,600
How is it that now, come home to your own house and goods,

180
00:12:06,600 --> 00:12:10,040
face to face with the suitors,
you whimper about being brave?

181
00:12:10,040 --> 00:12:15,980
Here, old friend — stand by me and watch the work,
and learn what kind of man, among enemy fighters,

182
00:12:15,980 --> 00:12:19,680
Mentor, Alcimus' son, is at repaying kindness."

183
00:12:19,680 --> 00:12:24,000
She spoke — and did not yet grant
victory outright to either side,

184
00:12:24,000 --> 00:12:28,240
but went on testing the strength and
fight of Odysseus and of his glorious son.

185
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:32,380
And she herself darted up to the
roof-beam of the smoke-stained hall

186
00:12:32,380 --> 00:12:35,480
and perched there — like a swallow, face-on.

187
00:12:35,480 --> 00:12:42,200
Now Agelaus, Damastor's son, rallied the suitors,
with Eurynomus, Amphimedon, Demoptolemus,

188
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:45,340
Peisander Polyctor's son, and Polybus the shrewd:

189
00:12:45,340 --> 00:12:51,060
for these were far the best of the suitors in prowess,
of all who still lived and fought for their lives —

190
00:12:51,060 --> 00:12:54,220
the rest the bow and the crowding arrows had brought down.

191
00:12:54,220 --> 00:12:58,380
And Agelaus spoke to them all,
putting the word to everyone:

192
00:12:58,380 --> 00:13:01,500
"Friends — now this man's invincible hands will stop.

193
00:13:01,500 --> 00:13:06,060
Mentor has left him — after the empty boasting —
and they are alone at the outer doors.

194
00:13:06,060 --> 00:13:08,540
So do not throw your long spears all together:

195
00:13:08,540 --> 00:13:14,720
come — you six throw first, and see if Zeus will
grant that Odysseus be hit, and we win the glory.

196
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:17,940
The rest are no concern, once that man falls."

197
00:13:17,940 --> 00:13:24,220
So he spoke; and they all threw as he ordered,
hungry for it. And Athena made every cast come to nothing:

198
00:13:24,220 --> 00:13:29,760
one man's spear struck the doorpost of the strong-built hall,
another's the close-fitted door itself,

199
00:13:29,760 --> 00:13:33,640
another's ash shaft, heavy with
bronze, slammed into the wall.

200
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:39,440
And when they had weathered the suitors' spears,
much-enduring noble Odysseus began among his men:

201
00:13:39,440 --> 00:13:41,880
"Friends — now it is mine to say it: throw!

202
00:13:41,880 --> 00:13:46,920
Into the crowd of the suitors — men wild to strip
our corpses on top of the wrongs they did before."

203
00:13:46,920 --> 00:13:53,300
So he spoke; and they all threw their sharp spears,
aiming straight ahead: Odysseus killed Demoptolemus;

204
00:13:53,300 --> 00:13:59,560
Telemachus, Euryades; the swineherd, Elatus;
and the man in charge of the cattle killed Peisander.

205
00:13:59,560 --> 00:14:02,520
All of those together bit the vast floor with their teeth,

206
00:14:02,520 --> 00:14:04,960
and the suitors fell back into the deep of the hall;

207
00:14:04,960 --> 00:14:08,620
and the others charged,
and pulled their spears from the corpses.

208
00:14:08,620 --> 00:14:14,180
Again the suitors threw their sharp spears, hungry for it;
and Athena made the many casts come to nothing:

209
00:14:14,180 --> 00:14:19,560
one man's spear struck the doorpost of the strong-built hall,
another's the close-fitted door itself,

210
00:14:19,560 --> 00:14:23,580
another's ash shaft, heavy with
bronze, slammed into the wall.

211
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:29,540
But Amphimedon caught Telemachus on the wrist —
a graze: the bronze just broke the surface of the skin;

212
00:14:29,540 --> 00:14:32,200
and Ctesippus, over the shield, scratched Eumaeus

213
00:14:32,200 --> 00:14:36,020
on the shoulder with his long spear:
it skipped and fell to earth.

214
00:14:36,020 --> 00:14:39,880
Then those around Odysseus —
the deep mind, the shifting counsels —

215
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:42,400
threw their sharp spears into the crowd of the suitors:

216
00:14:42,400 --> 00:14:46,480
and this time Eurydamas fell
to Odysseus, sacker of cities;

217
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:50,180
Amphimedon to Telemachus; Polybus to the swineherd;

218
00:14:50,180 --> 00:14:53,440
and then Ctesippus fell to the
man in charge of the cattle,

219
00:14:53,440 --> 00:14:56,380
struck full in the chest; and he crowed over him:

220
00:14:56,380 --> 00:15:00,060
"Son of Polytherses — you lover
of a good jeer! — never again

221
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:05,920
give way to your folly and talk big. Leave the
word to the gods: they are far stronger than you.

222
00:15:05,920 --> 00:15:09,020
This is your guest-gift in return — to match the ox-hoof

223
00:15:09,020 --> 00:15:12,940
you gave godlike Odysseus once,
when he begged through his own hall."

224
00:15:12,940 --> 00:15:16,380
So spoke the herdsman of the
spiral-horned cattle. And Odysseus

225
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:23,620
wounded Damastor's son, close in, with the long spear;
and Telemachus wounded Leocritus, Evenor's son,

226
00:15:23,620 --> 00:15:26,860
clean in the flank with his spear,
and drove the bronze through:

227
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:30,360
he pitched forward and struck the
ground full with his forehead.

228
00:15:30,360 --> 00:15:36,540
And now Athena raised the aegis that wastes mankind,
high from the roof; and their wits scattered in terror,

229
00:15:36,540 --> 00:15:39,580
and they stampeded through the hall like a herd of cattle

230
00:15:39,580 --> 00:15:43,000
when the darting gadfly falls on them and whirls them wild

231
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:46,780
in the season of spring, when the long days come in.

232
00:15:46,780 --> 00:15:50,580
And the others — like vultures
with hooked claws and curving beaks

233
00:15:50,580 --> 00:15:53,760
that come down out of the mountains on the smaller birds:

234
00:15:53,760 --> 00:16:00,320
these skim the plain, cowering away from the clouds,
but the vultures stoop and kill them; no defense,

235
00:16:00,320 --> 00:16:03,440
no flight — and men delight to watch the hunting:

236
00:16:03,440 --> 00:16:06,940
so those four, rushing the suitors through the house,

237
00:16:06,940 --> 00:16:10,060
struck them down left and right; and hideous groaning rose

238
00:16:10,060 --> 00:16:13,940
as heads were smashed, and the
whole floor smoked with blood.

239
00:16:13,940 --> 00:16:21,100
But Leodes ran in and caught Odysseus by the knees,
and clasping them in supplication spoke winged words:

240
00:16:21,100 --> 00:16:24,760
"By your knees, Odysseus — respect me, pity me!

241
00:16:24,760 --> 00:16:31,000
I tell you I never said a word to any woman in your halls,
never did one reckless thing — no: I kept trying

242
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:34,840
to stop the others, the suitors,
when one would do such things.

243
00:16:34,840 --> 00:16:38,280
But they would not listen,
would not hold their hands from evil,

244
00:16:38,280 --> 00:16:41,500
and so through their own recklessness
they have met an ugly fate.

245
00:16:41,500 --> 00:16:44,780
And I, their sacrifice-diviner, having done nothing,

246
00:16:44,780 --> 00:16:48,900
am to lie down with them: no thanks,
hereafter, for good service!"

247
00:16:48,900 --> 00:16:53,520
Then, glaring from under his brows,
Odysseus of the many designs replied:

248
00:16:53,520 --> 00:16:56,420
"If you declare yourself their sacrifice-diviner,

249
00:16:56,420 --> 00:16:59,620
then many a time, no doubt,
you must have prayed in my halls

250
00:16:59,620 --> 00:17:03,380
that the accomplishment of my
sweet homecoming be far away —

251
00:17:03,380 --> 00:17:06,500
that my dear wife go with you, and bear you children.

252
00:17:06,500 --> 00:17:09,740
For that you shall not slip
beneath a death that lays men low."

253
00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:15,220
So speaking, he took up in his strong hand a sword
that lay there — Agelaus had dropped it to the ground

254
00:17:15,220 --> 00:17:18,500
as he was killed — and drove it
through the middle of his neck:

255
00:17:18,500 --> 00:17:22,180
and while he was still speaking,
his head mixed with the dust.

256
00:17:22,180 --> 00:17:26,380
But the singer still dodged the black doom — Terpes' son,

257
00:17:26,380 --> 00:17:29,840
Phemius, who sang among the suitors under compulsion.

258
00:17:29,840 --> 00:17:36,300
He stood with the clear lyre in his hands close
by the side-door, and his mind was split two ways:

259
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:39,220
whether to slip out of the hall to the great altar of Zeus

260
00:17:39,220 --> 00:17:45,120
of the Courtyard, and sit there on the masoned
stone where Laertes and Odysseus had burned so many

261
00:17:45,120 --> 00:17:49,660
thighbones of oxen — or rush
forward and clasp Odysseus' knees.

262
00:17:49,660 --> 00:17:53,020
And as he weighed it, this seemed to him the better way:

263
00:17:53,020 --> 00:17:56,580
to take hold of the knees of Laertes' son Odysseus.

264
00:17:56,580 --> 00:18:02,300
So he set the hollow lyre down on the ground between
the mixing-bowl and the silver-studded chair,

265
00:18:02,300 --> 00:18:06,020
and rushed forward himself and
caught Odysseus by the knees,

266
00:18:06,020 --> 00:18:09,720
and clasping them in supplication spoke winged words:

267
00:18:09,720 --> 00:18:13,080
"By your knees, Odysseus — respect me, pity me!

268
00:18:13,080 --> 00:18:19,380
On you yourself the grief will fall hereafter,
if you kill a singer — me — who sing for gods and for men.

269
00:18:19,380 --> 00:18:25,660
I am self-taught: a god has planted in my heart all
the pathways of song; and I am fit to sing beside you

270
00:18:25,660 --> 00:18:29,340
as beside a god. Do not be hungry to cut my throat.

271
00:18:29,340 --> 00:18:32,520
And Telemachus would tell you this — your own dear son:

272
00:18:32,520 --> 00:18:35,500
that it was never by my will, never for want of mine,

273
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:38,560
that I came to your house to sing at the suitors' feasts:

274
00:18:38,560 --> 00:18:42,480
they were more, and stronger —
they marched me here by force."

275
00:18:42,480 --> 00:18:48,340
And the sacred strength of Telemachus heard him,
and quickly spoke to his father, standing near:

276
00:18:48,340 --> 00:18:51,900
"Stop! He is guiltless — do not cut him with the bronze.

277
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:57,200
And Medon the herald let us save as well,
who always cared for me in our house when I was a child —

278
00:18:57,200 --> 00:19:03,000
unless Philoetius has killed him already, or the swineherd,
or he met you as you stormed along the hall."

279
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:09,460
So he spoke; and Medon heard him — a man of sensible
mind — for he lay cowering under a chair, wrapped up

280
00:19:09,460 --> 00:19:12,880
in a fresh-flayed oxhide, dodging the black doom.

281
00:19:12,880 --> 00:19:16,920
Up from under the chair he came
at once, stripped off the oxhide,

282
00:19:16,920 --> 00:19:22,940
and rushed forward and caught Telemachus by the knees,
and clasping them in supplication spoke winged words:

283
00:19:22,940 --> 00:19:27,000
"Friend — here I am: myself!
Hold back, and tell your father,

284
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:30,720
before in his overmastering strength
he cuts me down with the sharp bronze

285
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:33,440
in his rage at the suitor-men — the men who sheared away

286
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:37,520
his goods in the halls,
and honored you not at all, the fools."

287
00:19:37,520 --> 00:19:40,920
And Odysseus of the many designs smiled at him and said:

288
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:44,720
"Take heart: this man has pulled you clear and saved you —

289
00:19:44,720 --> 00:19:50,980
so you may know in your heart, and tell another too,
how doing good outruns doing evil, and by far.

290
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:53,480
Now out of the hall with you, and sit in the courtyard,

291
00:19:53,480 --> 00:19:57,040
away from the slaughter —
you and the singer of many songs —

292
00:19:57,040 --> 00:19:59,680
while I finish in the house the work that I must."

293
00:19:59,680 --> 00:20:05,140
So he spoke; and the two of them went out of the
hall and sat down by the altar of great Zeus,

294
00:20:05,140 --> 00:20:08,240
peering about on every side, expecting death each moment.

295
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:14,840
And Odysseus peered through his own house, for any man
still living, hiding there, dodging the black doom.

296
00:20:14,840 --> 00:20:21,040
But he saw them all, the whole count of them,
in blood and dust fallen — so many, like fish that fishermen

297
00:20:21,040 --> 00:20:26,860
have hauled from the gray sea in the mesh of a net
onto the curved beach: heaped on the sand they lie,

298
00:20:26,860 --> 00:20:32,360
all of them, aching for the waves of the sea,
and the shining Sun takes the life out of them:

299
00:20:32,360 --> 00:20:34,840
so the suitors lay heaped on one another.

300
00:20:34,840 --> 00:20:37,880
Then Odysseus of the many designs said to Telemachus:

301
00:20:37,880 --> 00:20:43,460
"Telemachus — go, call me the nurse Eurycleia,
so I can tell her the word that is on my mind."

302
00:20:43,460 --> 00:20:49,720
So he spoke; and Telemachus obeyed his dear father,
and rattling the door he called the nurse Eurycleia:

303
00:20:49,720 --> 00:20:55,920
"Up with you, and come, old woman born long ago —
you who are overseer of our serving-women in the halls.

304
00:20:55,920 --> 00:20:59,200
Come: my father calls you; he has something to say."

305
00:20:59,200 --> 00:21:02,720
So he spoke; and for her the word stayed wingless.

306
00:21:02,720 --> 00:21:08,680
She opened the doors of the well-settled halls
and came; and Telemachus led the way before her.

307
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:13,720
And she found Odysseus among the bodies of the slain,
spattered with blood and gore, like a lion

308
00:21:13,720 --> 00:21:16,760
that comes from feeding on an ox of the farmstead:

309
00:21:16,760 --> 00:21:22,720
all his chest, and his cheeks on either side,
are bloody, and he is terrible to look in the face:

310
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:26,980
so was Odysseus spattered — feet, and hands above them.

311
00:21:26,980 --> 00:21:30,160
And she — when she saw the corpses and the sea of blood,

312
00:21:30,160 --> 00:21:34,060
she opened her mouth to raise
the cry of triumph: such a work

313
00:21:34,060 --> 00:21:38,740
she saw accomplished. But Odysseus
checked her, and held her back,

314
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:42,120
hot as she was, and spoke winged words to her:

315
00:21:42,120 --> 00:21:46,140
"In your own heart rejoice,
old woman — and hold it in: no cries.

316
00:21:46,140 --> 00:21:48,880
It is unholy to glory over slaughtered men.

317
00:21:48,880 --> 00:21:52,940
These men the doom of the gods has broken,
and their own ruthless deeds:

318
00:21:52,940 --> 00:21:57,660
for they honored nobody on this earth,
bad man or good, whoever came among them —

319
00:21:57,660 --> 00:22:01,520
and so through their own recklessness
they have met an ugly fate.

320
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:04,320
But come now, name me the women of the halls:

321
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:07,600
which of them dishonor me, and which are guiltless."

322
00:22:07,600 --> 00:22:10,060
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia answered him:

323
00:22:10,060 --> 00:22:12,800
"Then I will tell you, child, the whole truth of it.

324
00:22:12,800 --> 00:22:17,300
You have fifty serving-women in your halls —
slave-women we have taught to work their work,

325
00:22:17,300 --> 00:22:20,120
to card the wool and to bear the lot of slavery.

326
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:23,740
Of these, twelve in all have
walked the road of shamelessness:

327
00:22:23,740 --> 00:22:27,180
no respect for me — none for Penelope herself.

328
00:22:27,180 --> 00:22:32,420
Telemachus is only newly grown; his mother would
not let him give commands to the serving-women.

329
00:22:32,420 --> 00:22:38,240
But come — let me climb to the bright upper room and
tell your wife. Some god has laid a sleep on her."

330
00:22:38,240 --> 00:22:41,000
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

331
00:22:41,000 --> 00:22:47,340
"Do not wake her yet. Tell them to come here —
the women who all this while contrived their shamelessness."

332
00:22:47,340 --> 00:22:52,640
So he spoke; and the old woman went off down the
hall to carry word to the women and hurry them along.

333
00:22:52,640 --> 00:22:58,940
And he called Telemachus to his side, and the cowherd,
and the swineherd, and spoke winged words to them:

334
00:22:58,940 --> 00:23:04,020
"Begin carrying out the dead, and set the women to it;
and after that, the beautiful chairs and the tables —

335
00:23:04,020 --> 00:23:06,860
have them cleaned with water and porous sponges.

336
00:23:06,860 --> 00:23:10,980
And when you have set the whole house in order,
lead the serving-women out of the strong-built hall

337
00:23:10,980 --> 00:23:14,560
to the spot between the roundhouse
and the good courtyard fence,

338
00:23:14,560 --> 00:23:17,460
and cut them down with the long
swords, till you have stripped

339
00:23:17,460 --> 00:23:20,760
the life from all of them,
and they have forgotten Aphrodite —

340
00:23:20,760 --> 00:23:24,420
the sweetness they took under
the suitors, coupling in secret."

341
00:23:24,420 --> 00:23:30,680
So he spoke; and the women came in a huddle, all of them,
wailing terribly, the big tears streaming down.

342
00:23:30,680 --> 00:23:36,040
First they carried out the corpses of the dead and laid
them under the portico of the well-fenced courtyard,

343
00:23:36,040 --> 00:23:39,240
propping them against each other; and Odysseus commanded,

344
00:23:39,240 --> 00:23:42,760
hurrying the work himself;
and they carried them out — under force.

345
00:23:42,760 --> 00:23:47,540
And after that they cleaned the beautiful chairs
and the tables with water and porous sponges;

346
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:53,060
and Telemachus, with the cowherd and the swineherd,
scraped the floor of the strong-founded house

347
00:23:53,060 --> 00:23:56,940
with hoes, and the slave-women
carried the scrapings out of doors.

348
00:23:56,940 --> 00:24:01,760
And when they had set the whole hall in order,
they led the serving-women out of the strong-built hall

349
00:24:01,760 --> 00:24:05,760
to the spot between the roundhouse
and the good courtyard fence,

350
00:24:05,760 --> 00:24:08,640
and penned them in the narrow place,
where there was no way out.

351
00:24:08,640 --> 00:24:12,680
And clear-headed Telemachus began, and spoke among them:

352
00:24:12,680 --> 00:24:17,660
"By no clean death will I take the lives of these —
these who poured their insults on my head,

353
00:24:17,660 --> 00:24:21,060
on my own mother, and slept beside the suitors."

354
00:24:21,060 --> 00:24:24,220
So he spoke; and he made the cable of a dark-prowed ship

355
00:24:24,220 --> 00:24:27,660
fast to a great pillar, and flung it round the roundhouse,

356
00:24:27,660 --> 00:24:31,400
stretched taut, up high,
so no foot could reach the ground.

357
00:24:31,400 --> 00:24:37,520
And as when thrushes with their long wings,
or doves, strike into a snare set up in a thicket,

358
00:24:37,520 --> 00:24:40,820
heading in to roost, and a hateful bed receives them:

359
00:24:40,820 --> 00:24:43,900
so the women held their heads in a row, and round every

360
00:24:43,900 --> 00:24:47,820
neck a noose was laid, that they might die most pitiably.

361
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:51,300
They kicked out with their feet
a little while — not very long.

362
00:24:51,300 --> 00:24:54,620
And Melanthius they dragged out
through the porch and the courtyard:

363
00:24:54,620 --> 00:24:58,240
they sliced away his nose and
ears with the pitiless bronze

364
00:24:58,240 --> 00:25:01,940
and tore out his genitals, raw meat for the dogs to share,

365
00:25:01,940 --> 00:25:05,020
and lopped off his hands and feet in their fury of heart.

366
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:10,800
Then they washed their own hands and feet and went
into the house to Odysseus: the work was accomplished.

367
00:25:10,800 --> 00:25:13,480
And he spoke to the dear nurse Eurycleia:

368
00:25:13,480 --> 00:25:17,260
"Bring sulfur, old woman —
the cure of evils — and bring me fire,

369
00:25:17,260 --> 00:25:22,460
so I can fume the hall with it. And bid Penelope
come down to me, with her attendant women;

370
00:25:22,460 --> 00:25:25,620
and rouse all the serving-women in the house to come."

371
00:25:25,620 --> 00:25:28,040
Then the dear nurse Eurycleia answered him:

372
00:25:28,040 --> 00:25:31,560
"Yes, my child — all this you have said as it should be.

373
00:25:31,560 --> 00:25:35,300
But come, let me bring you clothes, a cloak and a tunic:

374
00:25:35,300 --> 00:25:38,340
do not stand in your halls like this,
with your broad shoulders

375
00:25:38,340 --> 00:25:41,760
wrapped in rags. That would be a thing to blame."

376
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:44,500
Then Odysseus of the many designs answered her:

377
00:25:44,500 --> 00:25:47,560
"First of all: let there be fire for me in the hall."

378
00:25:47,560 --> 00:25:53,820
So he spoke; and the dear nurse Eurycleia did not disobey,
but brought the fire and the sulfur; and Odysseus

379
00:25:53,820 --> 00:25:57,340
fumed through and through the hall,
the house, the courtyard.

380
00:25:57,340 --> 00:26:00,740
And the old woman went off up through
the beautiful house of Odysseus

381
00:26:00,740 --> 00:26:07,140
to carry word to the women and hurry them along;
and out of the hall they came, torches in their hands,

382
00:26:07,140 --> 00:26:13,100
and they poured around Odysseus, and embraced him,
and kissed his head and his shoulders, welcoming him,

383
00:26:13,100 --> 00:26:16,560
catching and clasping his hands;
and a sweet longing seized him

384
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:20,112
to weep and to sob: for in his heart he knew them all.
